  "articleBody": "It can be really frustrating when you've put effort into your website, but AI Overviews or answer engines just aren't citing your business. The truth is, AI systems don't \"read\" like humans do, and they have specific needs that traditional websites often don't meet. You might have great content, but if it's not structured correctly, or if your site has technical hurdles, AI might pass right over it.\n\nFirst off, don't assume there's a special \"AI index\" or secret markup. Google's pretty clear: your pages just need to meet standard search technical requirements, be indexed, and be eligible for a search snippet. The real game-changer is how your content is presented. AI loves clear, fact-dense, and semantically relevant information that directly answers questions. If your site is packed with heavy JavaScript or relies on images for key info, it's a no-go for AI crawlers.\n\nAnother common issue is content quality. Low-value or inaccurate machine-generated pages, especially in large volumes, can actually hurt you. Google's guidelines emphasize accuracy, relevance, and value, not just the method of production. Your content needs to be people-first, useful, and original.\n\nWhat to Do Next:\n\nIf you're scratching your head wondering why AI isn't picking up your content, start by checking your site's basics. Is it crawlable? Is it indexed? Is your important information presented as plain text? Are you systematically answering the real questions your customers are asking? If you're a local service business or a law firm, focusing on those specific \"near me\" or scenario-based questions is crucial. Sometimes, a full audit is needed to identify deeper issues. If you're running a complex site or have tried these steps without success, bringing in a specialist could save you a lot of headaches.",
  "datePublished": "2026-09-23"
